<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal.BroilerJs</RootNamespace>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <Description>The Broiler.JS provider for JSEAL: an IJsEngineProvider and IJsRealm implemented over Broiler.JavaScript's JSContext, so the HTML bridge can bind a document through the engine-neutral contracts without changing which engine actually runs the page.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The realm's job pump and marshalling internals are what a conformance suite has to
         reach to assert that the provider answers the contract the way the bridge needs. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Broiler.Browser.Core.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The contracts this assembly implements. Everything public here is a JSEAL type. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal\Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal.csproj" />

    <!-- JSContext (the realm and its global), JSEngine (the ambient-realm scope the engine's
         intrinsic factories read), and Eval. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.JS\Broiler.JS\Broiler.JavaScript.Engine\Broiler.JavaScript.Engine.csproj" />

    <!-- The value types the marshal names: JSFunction, JSArray, JSString, JSNumber, JSBoolean,
         JSNull, and JSPromise. JSObject/JSValue/KeyString arrive transitively through Runtime
         and Storage, which this project deliberately does not name: it uses no type from either
         that BuiltIns does not already put in its own signatures. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.JS\Broiler.JS\Broiler.JavaScript.BuiltIns\Broiler.JavaScript.BuiltIns.csproj" />

    <!--
      JSGlobalStatic.StructuredClone, and nothing else. IJsClone is the contract half of
      JsCapabilities.WorkerRealms — "values moved between the two realms by structured clone" — and
      Broiler.JS implements the algorithm here, in Globals.

      THE TWO ALTERNATIVES WERE BOTH WORSE, which is the argument this reference has to make because
      it raises this project's engineProjectRefs from 2 to 3 and eng/jseal-budget.json enforces that
      number even for a provider.

        * Reimplementing structured clone inside this project would be a SECOND definition of which
          types survive a clone — Date, RegExp, Map, Set, ArrayBuffer, the typed arrays, Error,
          circular references — and it would drift from the engine's. The drift would surface as a
          value that crossed to a Worker in a shape the same page could not produce at home.
        * Calling the realm's own structuredClone global would need no reference, and does not
          work: the engine gates that export behind JavaScriptFeatureFlags.StructuredClone, an
          experimental ES2026 flag that is off by default, so typeof structuredClone is
          "undefined" on both a context this provider builds and the one the host hands it to adopt.
          The static entry point is not gated, which is why the DOM bridge always called it directly.

      It is a reference that MOVES rather than one that is added. Broiler.HtmlBridge.Dom referenced
      Broiler.JavaScript.Globals for exactly this — JSWorker, WorkerBinding and MessagingBinding each
      called JSGlobalStatic.StructuredClone — and no source under that project names it any more, so
      its own ProjectReference is now unused and its engineProjectRefs can fall from 4 to 3 in the
      same commit that raises this project from 2 to 3. A provider naming its engine is what a
      provider is for; a binding naming it is what the budget exists to count down.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.JS\Broiler.JS\Broiler.JavaScript.Globals\Broiler.JavaScript.Globals.csproj" />

    <!-- Broiler.JavaScript.Modules is deliberately ABSENT, unlike Broiler.HtmlBridge.Scripting's
         reference block which this one is modelled on. A module-bearing realm is what the
         Modules/DynamicImport capabilities describe, and this provider declares neither by
         default — see the comment on BroilerJsEngineProvider.Capabilities. Referencing the
         module assembly to implement a capability that is not asserted would link a module
         orchestrator into every host that only wanted a document. -->
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
